40
00:01:12,129 --> 00:01:14,449
The model's input consists of an image and a piece of text

41
00:01:14,449 --> 00:01:16,870
The model's output is also a piece of text, which is very simple.

42
00:01:16,870 --> 00:01:18,930
However, the input includes both images and text

43
00:01:18,930 --> 00:01:20,310
They are in different forms.

44
00:01:20,310 --> 00:01:21,610
The difficulty lies right here

45
00:01:21,610 --> 00:01:24,470
We call this type of model that contains different types of information

46
00:01:24,470 --> 00:01:25,910
called multimodal

47
00:01:25,910 --> 00:01:27,270
Let's simplify it first

48
00:01:27,270 --> 00:01:27,930
remove the image

49
00:01:27,930 --> 00:01:28,830
only text remains

50
00:01:28,830 --> 00:01:30,910
then this process is simply an ordinary

51
00:01:30,910 --> 00:01:33,180
a large language model implemented based on the Transformer architecture

52
00:01:33,180 --> 00:01:34,780
first perform tokenization on this text

53
00:01:34,780 --> 00:01:37,120
then convert each word into a vector and input it into the model

54
00:01:37,120 --> 00:01:39,280
Finally, the model outputs the probability distribution of the vocabulary.

55
00:01:39,280 --> 00:01:41,070
thereby obtaining the predicted next word

56
00:01:41,070 --> 00:01:43,530
Add the next word back to the end of the original text

57
00:01:43,530 --> 00:01:44,270
iteration

58
00:01:44,270 --> 00:01:45,270
continuously generating new words

59
00:01:45,270 --> 00:01:46,710
This forms a response

60
00:01:46,710 --> 00:01:49,060
Friends who are not familiar with this process can take a look at this video

61
00:01:49,060 --> 00:01:51,840
will guide you from scratch to gain a basic understanding of AI principles

62
00:01:51,840 --> 00:01:52,780
If you don't have time

63
00:01:52,780 --> 00:01:53,420
it's also fine

64
00:01:53,420 --> 00:01:56,509
Just treat the model as a black box, or a function.

65
00:01:56,509 --> 00:01:58,949
Okay, this modality is a text-based large language model

66
00:01:58,949 --> 00:02:00,349
After continuous efforts in recent years

67
00:02:00,349 --> 00:02:01,369
has already become very mature

68
00:02:01,369 --> 00:02:03,969
Then we just need to integrate the image into this model, and that's it.

69
00:02:03,969 --> 00:02:05,880
So how do we incorporate this image?

70
00:02:05,880 --> 00:02:08,660
The simplest idea is to first convert the image into a text

71
00:02:08,660 --> 00:02:11,039
Then just concatenate it with another piece of text, wouldn't that work?

72
00:02:11,039 --> 00:02:12,420
but the step of converting the image into text

73
00:02:12,420 --> 00:02:14,400
Isn't it still a multimodal task?

74
00:02:14,400 --> 00:02:15,570
still need to figure out a solution

75
00:02:15,570 --> 00:02:18,170
Let's take it a step further and convert the image into a set of vectors

76
00:02:18,170 --> 00:02:19,270
the same as the text part

77
00:02:19,270 --> 00:02:20,250
For the model

78
00:02:20,250 --> 00:02:22,210
The information I receive is just a bunch of vectors

79
00:02:22,210 --> 00:02:23,130
I don't care

80
00:02:23,130 --> 00:02:25,130
I don't know or care what you've been transformed from either

81
00:02:25,130 --> 00:02:27,870
Done! So how do you turn a picture into a set of vectors?

82
00:02:27,870 --> 00:02:29,510
This is nothing unusual.

83
00:02:29,510 --> 00:02:31,150
using traditional convolutional neural networks or

84
00:02:31,150 --> 00:02:32,970
or the ViT that emerged after the rise of Transformer

85
00:02:32,970 --> 00:02:34,890
can transform images into a set of vectors

86
00:02:34,890 --> 00:02:36,950
we generally refer to it as an image encoder

87
00:02:36,950 --> 00:02:38,890
There are also many methods for the text part

88
00:02:38,890 --> 00:02:40,710
we collectively refer to them as text encoders

89
00:02:40,710 --> 00:02:43,750
So can we just concatenate these two sets of vectors and feed them directly to the model?

90
00:02:43,750 --> 00:02:45,490
It's common sense that it's not possible

91
00:02:45,490 --> 00:02:46,630
In large language models

92
00:02:46,630 --> 00:02:49,530
Word vectors are trained to have semantic relationships between them

93
00:02:49,530 --> 00:02:50,030
Simply put,

94
00:02:50,030 --> 00:02:52,959
The word vector for 'man' and the word vector for 'woman' look quite similar

95
00:02:52,959 --> 00:02:53,919
expressed in mathematical terms

96
00:02:53,919 --> 00:02:56,579
that is, the dot product or cosine similarity between the two vectors is relatively large

97
00:02:56,579 --> 00:02:59,159
Then, in another model, the generated image vectors

98
00:02:59,159 --> 00:03:00,779
in the vector space of language models

99
00:03:00,779 --> 00:03:01,859
like a foreign language

100
00:03:01,859 --> 00:03:02,459
incompatible

101
00:03:02,459 --> 00:03:03,380
They just don't speak the same language.

102
00:03:03,380 --> 00:03:05,300
For example, in the vector space of large language models

103
00:03:05,300 --> 00:03:07,430
an image of a dog is converted into a set of vectors

104
00:03:07,430 --> 00:03:10,070
might instead be very close to a completely unrelated dog vector

105
00:03:10,070 --> 00:03:12,150
then the final answer would definitely be all messed up

106
00:03:12,150 --> 00:03:13,790
So this crucial step

107
00:03:13,790 --> 00:03:16,630
is to align images and text in the same vector space

108
00:03:16,630 --> 00:03:18,350
the vector converted from the image of a dog

109
00:03:18,350 --> 00:03:21,050
and the vector converted from the word 'dog' are similar

110
00:03:21,050 --> 00:03:22,730
This is called modality alignment

111
00:03:22,730 --> 00:03:26,070
That is, mapping the features of different modalities to the same semantic space

112
00:03:26,070 --> 00:03:27,990
So how do we achieve modality alignment?

113
00:03:27,990 --> 00:03:31,150
It's very simple. First, you go online and find 400 million pairs of images and text.

114
00:03:31,150 --> 00:03:33,060
Each image corresponds to a piece of text description

115
00:03:33,060 --> 00:03:36,500
Then, each image is processed by the aforementioned image encoder to generate vectors

116
00:03:36,500 --> 00:03:39,500
Each piece of text is also converted into a vector through the aforementioned text encoder.

117
00:03:39,500 --> 00:03:42,060
arrange them like this

118
00:03:43,620 --> 00:03:46,500
Next, perform a dot product between each image vector and text vector pairwise.

119
00:03:46,500 --> 00:03:48,020
that is, the similarity is calculated

120
00:03:48,020 --> 00:03:50,060
Pay attention to the set of results on the diagonal

121
00:03:50,060 --> 00:03:52,620
the dot product of the matched pair of post and image vectors

122
00:03:52,620 --> 00:03:54,780
We call these samples positive sample pairs

123
00:03:54,780 --> 00:03:56,720
and hope that their dot product is as large as possible

124
00:03:56,720 --> 00:04:00,000
The text pairs that do not match in other positions are called negative sample pairs.

125
00:04:00,000 --> 00:04:02,300
and hope their dot product is as small as possible

126
00:04:02,300 --> 00:04:04,100
This approach of bringing positive sample pairs closer

127
00:04:04,100 --> 00:04:08,000
The machine learning method of pushing negative sample pairs apart is called contrastive learning.

128
00:04:08,280 --> 00:04:09,240
With this method

129
00:04:09,240 --> 00:04:10,760
we can then define the loss function

130
00:04:10,760 --> 00:04:13,960
Then train for 18 days on 592 V100 GPUs

131
00:04:13,960 --> 00:04:15,020
the job is done

132
00:04:15,020 --> 00:04:16,209
Isn't it simple?

133
00:04:16,209 --> 00:04:17,329
This is OpenAI

134
00:04:17,329 --> 00:04:19,629
